---

<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="50–60 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Grammar · Lesson {page.sequence}</p>
  <h1>Verb + infinitive or <em>-ing: pattern, participant, and meaning</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Build the whole message, not only the second verb: <span class="ex">Maya plans to teach. She enjoys teaching. She asked Leo to test the projector. She decided not to cancel. The team looks forward to welcoming visitors.</span></p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">50–60 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">to + base · verb-ing</span>
    <span class="tag">person + to · not to · preposition + -ing</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Find the stored pattern and the performer of each action.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Separate infinitive <em>to</em> from prepositional <em>to</em>.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Control objects, negation, questions, and meaning changes.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Give a fictional workshop-planning update and answer follow-ups.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can use common <span class="ex">verb + to + base</span> and <span class="ex">verb + -ing</span> chunks; add a person who performs the second action; place negation accurately; distinguish infinitive <span class="ex">to</span> from prepositional <span class="ex">to</span>; and interpret useful meaning contrasts with <span class="ex">stop, remember</span>, and <span class="ex">try</span>.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved -ing spelling, third-person agreement, and a planned future form.">
    <h3>Three checks from earlier lessons</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use the active-now context, singular subject, and prior plan.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Earlier lesson choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="writing">writing</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="writeing">writeing</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="works">works</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="work">work</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="is going to">is going to</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="going to">going to</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Leo is in the middle of the action now: He is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="writing" data-hint="Write drops final e before -ing: writing." data-fix="Leo is writing the equipment list now." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the equipment list now.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The singular subject projector takes present-simple agreement: The projector <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="works" data-hint="A singular present-simple subject takes works." data-fix="The projector works after the restart." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> after the restart.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Maya formed this plan yesterday and still intends it: She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="is going to" data-hint="The prior plan uses be going to; singular she requires is." data-fix="She is going to teach an evening workshop." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> teach an evening workshop.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice six jobs inside one plan</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Linden Community Center workshop plan</h3>
    <p><b>Chosen action:</b> <span class="ex">Maya plans to teach an evening workshop.</span></p>
    <p><b>Activity as experience:</b> <span class="ex">She enjoys teaching adults.</span></p>
    <p><b>Another performer:</b> <span class="ex">She asked Leo to test the projector.</span></p>
    <p><b>Negative chosen action:</b> <span class="ex">She decided not to cancel the session.</span></p>
    <p><b>Preposition before an activity:</b> <span class="ex">The team looks forward to welcoming visitors.</span></p>
    <p><b>Activity ended:</b> <span class="ex">Leo stopped testing at six.</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which patterns use <span class="ex">to + base</span>, and which use <span class="ex">verb-ing</span>?</li>
      <li>Who will test the projector?</li>
      <li>Does the second <span class="ex">to</span> in <span class="ex">looks forward to welcoming</span> behave like infinitive <span class="ex">to</span>?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You identified a to-infinitive, -ing complement, person plus infinitive, negative infinitive, prepositional to, and ended activity.">
    <h3>Discovery check: identify the pattern’s job</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Verb pattern job choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="to-infinitive complement">to-infinitive complement</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="-ing complement">-ing complement</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="person plus to-infinitive">person plus to-infinitive</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="negative to-infinitive">negative to-infinitive</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="preposition plus -ing">preposition plus -ing</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="ended activity">ended activity</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>In <span class="ex">plans to teach</span>, <span class="ex">plan</span> selects a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="to-infinitive complement" data-hint="Plan is stored with to plus a base verb for the planned action." data-fix="Plans to teach contains a to-infinitive complement." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>In <span class="ex">enjoys teaching</span>, <span class="ex">enjoy</span> selects an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="-ing complement" data-hint="Enjoy is stored with an -ing form for the enjoyed activity." data-fix="Enjoys teaching contains an -ing complement." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>In <span class="ex">asked Leo to test</span>, Leo performs the testing, so the pattern is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="person plus to-infinitive" data-hint="The person Leo comes before to test and names the second action’s performer." data-fix="Asked Leo to test uses person plus to-infinitive." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>In <span class="ex">decided not to cancel</span>, Maya made a decision to keep the session, so the pattern is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="negative to-infinitive" data-hint="Not comes before to cancel to negate the chosen second action." data-fix="Decided not to cancel contains a negative to-infinitive." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>In <span class="ex">looks forward to welcoming</span>, <span class="ex">to</span> belongs to the expression <span class="ex">look forward to</span>, so the pattern is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="preposition plus -ing" data-hint="Here to is a preposition, and the following verb takes -ing." data-fix="Looks forward to welcoming uses preposition plus -ing." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>In <span class="ex">stopped testing at six</span>, testing ended at six, so <span class="ex">testing</span> names the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="ended activity" data-hint="Stop plus -ing means the named activity ends." data-fix="Stopped testing presents testing as the ended activity." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: store the whole pattern with the verb</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A first verb allows one or more shapes for the second verb or phrase that follows it. Learn the first verb with a complete chunk and meaning: <span class="ex">plan to open, enjoy teaching, ask Maya to wait, look forward to meeting</span>.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>common pattern</th><th>useful A2 verbs or expressions</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">verb + to + base</span></td><td>want, need, decide, hope, plan, learn, agree</td><td><span class="ex">They agreed to help.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">verb + verb-ing</span></td><td>avoid, enjoy, finish, keep, mind, practice</td><td><span class="ex">They finished checking the room.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">verb + person + to + base</span></td><td>ask, tell, want, would like</td><td><span class="ex">Maya asked Leo to test it.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">preposition + verb-ing</span></td><td>interested in, good at, thanks for, look forward to</td><td><span class="ex">We look forward to meeting you.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">A meaning tendency can help memory, but it cannot safely predict an unfamiliar verb. Goals often appear with <span class="ex">to</span>, and experiences often appear with <span class="ex">-ing</span>, but <span class="ex">avoid making</span> can describe a future goal and <span class="ex">learn to drive</span> can describe an experienced activity. Check and store the actual pattern.</p>

  <h3>Let the first verb carry tense, agreement, questions, and main negation</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>job</th><th>pattern</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>person and tense</td><td>change the first verb; keep the complement form</td><td><span class="ex">She plans to teach. She planned to teach.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>question</td><td>build the question around the first verb</td><td><span class="ex">Does she plan to teach?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>main negative</td><td>negate the first verb</td><td><span class="ex">She does not plan to cancel.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Do not add tense or agreement again to the second verb: <span class="ex">She wants to go</span>, not <span class="ex">she wants to goes</span>. An <span class="ex">-ing</span> complement does not need <span class="ex">be</span>: <span class="ex">She enjoys teaching</span>, not <span class="ex">she enjoys is teaching</span>.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You controlled to-infinitive and -ing chunks, first-verb question form, continued practice, desired action, and a future avoidance goal.">
    <h3>Drill 1: complete the stored core pattern</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Core verb pattern choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="to leave">to leave</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="leaving">leaving</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="checking">checking</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="to check">to check</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="plan to join">plan to join</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="plans to joins">plans to joins</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="keep practicing">keep practicing</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="keep to practice">keep to practice</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="to reserve">to reserve</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="reserving">reserving</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="touching">touching</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="to touch">to touch</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Leo wants an earlier departure, so he wants <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="to leave" data-hint="Want takes to plus a base verb for the desired action." data-fix="Leo wants to leave by six." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> by six.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The team completes the whole room check before lunch: They finish <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="checking" data-hint="Finish takes the -ing form for the completed activity." data-fix="They finish checking the room before lunch." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the room before lunch.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Ask about Maya’s current plan. After <span class="ex">Does Maya</span>, use the base form of the first and second verbs: Does Maya <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="plan to join" data-hint="Does carries the agreement, so use plan; infinitive to is followed by base join." data-fix="Does Maya plan to join the session?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the session?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The tutor requests continued repeated practice: Please <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="keep practicing" data-hint="Keep takes an -ing complement for continued activity." data-fix="Please keep practicing the introduction." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the introduction.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The caller expresses a polite desire for an action: I’d like <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="to reserve" data-hint="When would like introduces a desired action, use to plus the base verb." data-fix="I’d like to reserve the room, please." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the room, please.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The safety goal is to prevent future contact with the hot surface: Avoid <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="touching" data-hint="Avoid takes an -ing form even when the avoided action is in the future." data-fix="Avoid touching the hot surface." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the hot surface.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Name the second performer and place negation precisely</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>With <span class="ex">ask, tell, want</span>, and action uses of <span class="ex">would like</span>, a person can appear before the to-infinitive. That person performs the second action.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>message</th><th>pattern</th><th>performer of second action</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">Maya plans to test it.</span></td><td>same subject + to-infinitive</td><td>Maya</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">Maya asked Leo to test it.</span></td><td>verb + person + to-infinitive</td><td>Leo</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">Maya told Leo not to open it.</span></td><td>verb + person + not + to-infinitive</td><td>Leo avoids opening</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>Main-clause negation and complement negation do not say the same thing:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>sentence</th><th>meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">Maya did not decide to cancel.</span></td><td>there was no decision to cancel; another outcome may still be open</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">Maya decided not to cancel.</span></td><td>there was a decision, and its chosen action was to keep the session</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Two kinds of <em>to</em></h3>
  <p>Infinitive <span class="ex">to</span> is followed by a base verb: <span class="ex">plan to meet</span>. Prepositional <span class="ex">to</span> belongs to an expression and follows the same pattern as other prepositions. If a verb follows that preposition, use <span class="ex">-ing</span>: <span class="ex">look forward to meeting</span>. The reliable rule is not “every <span class="ex">to</span> takes a base verb.”</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You identified the second performer, object negation, complement negation, main-clause negation, and two preposition-plus-ing patterns.">
    <h3>Drill 2: build participant, negation, and preposition meaning</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Participant and negation choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Leo to test">Leo to test</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="that Leo tests">that Leo tests</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Leo not to open">Leo not to open</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Leo to not opening">Leo to not opening</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="not to cancel">not to cancel</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="to not canceling">to not canceling</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="did not decide to cancel">did not decide to cancel</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="decided not cancel">decided not cancel</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="learning">learning</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="to learn">to learn</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="meeting">meeting</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="meet">meet</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Leo, not Maya, will perform the test: Maya asked <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Leo to test" data-hint="Put the second performer Leo before to test." data-fix="Maya asked Leo to test the projector." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the projector.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Leo receives an instruction to avoid opening the cabinet: Maya told <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Leo not to open" data-hint="The person comes before not to plus the prohibited action." data-fix="Maya told Leo not to open the cabinet." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the cabinet.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Maya made a decision, and the decision was to keep the session: She decided <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="not to cancel" data-hint="Negate the chosen second action with not before to cancel." data-fix="She decided not to cancel the session." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the session.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The report says no cancellation decision was reached; it does not report a firm decision to keep the session: Maya <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="did not decide to cancel" data-hint="Negate the first verb decide to say that no decision to cancel was reached." data-fix="Maya did not decide to cancel the session." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the session.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>After the preposition <span class="ex">in</span>, the following verb uses <span class="ex">-ing</span>: Leo is interested in <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="learning" data-hint="When a verb follows the preposition in, use the -ing form." data-fix="Leo is interested in learning about sound systems." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> about sound systems.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>In <span class="ex">look forward to</span>, <span class="ex">to</span> is a preposition: The team looks forward to <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="meeting" data-hint="Prepositional to in look forward to is followed by meeting, not the base form meet." data-fix="The team looks forward to meeting the visitors." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the visitors.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>When both shapes are possible, check the meaning</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Some verbs license both shapes. The result may overlap, or the pattern may change the relationship between the two actions.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>verb group</th><th>useful distinction</th><th>examples</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>like, love, hate, prefer</td><td><span class="ex">-ing</span> often foregrounds experience or enjoyment; <span class="ex">to</span> can foreground a habit, choice, or considered action. Many contexts overlap.</td><td><span class="ex">I like swimming. I like to check the doors before leaving.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>start, begin, continue</td><td>both forms are often possible with little practical difference</td><td><span class="ex">It started raining. It started to rain.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>would like + action</td><td>desired action uses <span class="ex">to + base</span></td><td><span class="ex">I’d like to reserve a room.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">Do not force a difference where context does not support one, and do not claim the forms are always identical. Learn a complete sentence, the speaker’s purpose, and any contrast together.</p>

  <h3>Three high-value meaning contrasts</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>pattern</th><th>relationship between actions</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">stop doing</span></td><td>end the named activity</td><td><span class="ex">Leo stopped testing at six.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">stop to do</span></td><td>pause another activity in order to do this new action</td><td><span class="ex">Leo stopped to answer the phone.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">remember to do</span></td><td>remember a responsibility, then perform it</td><td><span class="ex">He remembered to lock the cabinet.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">remember doing</span></td><td>have a memory of a past action</td><td><span class="ex">He remembers locking the cabinet.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">try to do</span></td><td>make an effort to achieve the action</td><td><span class="ex">He tried to restart the projector.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">try doing</span></td><td>use the action as a possible method or experiment</td><td><span class="ex">He tried restarting the projector.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You distinguished an ended activity, a purpose after pausing, a completed responsibility, a past memory, an effort, and an experimental method.">
    <h3>Drill 3: choose the relationship between two actions</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Meaning contrast choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="stopped testing">stopped testing</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="stopped to test">stopped to test</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="stopped to answer">stopped to answer</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="stopped answering">stopped answering</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="remembered to lock">remembered to lock</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="remembered locking">remembered locking</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="remembers locking">remembers locking</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="remembers to lock">remembers to lock</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="tried to restart">tried to restart</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="tried restarting">tried restarting</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>At 6:00, Leo ended the projector test and went home: He <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="stopped testing" data-hint="Stop plus -ing names the activity that ended." data-fix="Leo stopped testing at six." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> at six.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>While testing, Leo heard the phone, paused the test, and answered it: He <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="stopped to answer" data-hint="Stop plus to-infinitive names the new purpose after pausing another action." data-fix="Leo stopped to answer the phone." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the phone.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The cabinet is secure because Leo did not forget the responsibility: He <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="remembered to lock" data-hint="Remember to do means the responsibility was remembered and performed." data-fix="Leo remembered to lock the cabinet." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the cabinet.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Today Leo can picture yesterday’s past locking action in his memory: He <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="remembers locking" data-hint="Remember doing presents a memory of a past action." data-fix="Leo remembers locking the cabinet yesterday." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the cabinet yesterday.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Leo made an effort to restart the projector, but it did not respond: He <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="tried to restart" data-hint="Try to do focuses on the effort to achieve an action." data-fix="Leo tried to restart the projector, but it did not respond." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the projector.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>Maya suggested one possible solution, so Leo used a restart as an experiment: He <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="tried restarting" data-hint="Try doing presents the action as a possible method or experiment." data-fix="Leo tried restarting the projector, and the picture returned." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the projector, and the picture returned.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: weaken infinitive <em>to</em>, keep <em>-ing</em> nasal, and stress the contrast</h3>
    <p>Infinitive <span class="ex">to</span> is commonly weak /tə/: <span class="ex">plan to teach /ˈplæn tə ˈtitʃ/</span>, <span class="ex">I’d like to reserve /aɪd ˈlaɪk tə rɪˈzɝv/</span>. The action usually carries the strongest beat.</p>
    <p>In fast casual U.S. speech, <span class="ex">want to</span> can sound like /ˈwɑnə/: <span class="ex">I want to leave</span>. Keep the standard spelling. Do not use that reduction to remove a person: <span class="ex">I want Leo to leave</span> needs Leo before <span class="ex">to leave</span>.</p>
    <p>The <span class="ex">-ing</span> ending finishes with /ɪŋ/, not a separate hard /g/: <span class="ex">working /ˈwɝkɪŋ/</span>. Stress can expose a meaning contrast: <span class="ex">He stopped <strong>WORK</strong>ing</span>, but <span class="ex">He stopped to <strong>AN</strong>swer</span>.</p>
  </div>

